Architecture plan for Issue #236: Show "ideavibes blog" as the header/footer brand lockup

Scope and module structure
- Shared presentation templates:
  - `rust-backend/templates/partials/header.html` owns the public header brand link and primary navigation rendering.
  - `rust-backend/templates/partials/footer.html` owns the footer brand link, tagline, and newsletter form.
  - Both partials currently render the hosted `https://ideavibes.ai/logo.png` image alone. The implementation should keep that image (`h-8 w-auto`) and add visible adjacent text so the lockup reads `ideavibes blog`.
- Shared layout/render tests:
  - `rust-backend/src/html/mod.rs` contains assertions for the rendered shared public layout. Update the assertions that expect `alt="Ideavibes"` and `aria-label="Ideavibes home"` to expect the new `ideavibes blog` accessibility text and visible wordmark.
- CSS/design organization:
  - Use existing Tailwind utility classes in the partials. No new stylesheet, Tailwind config, or component abstraction is needed for this small visual lockup change.
- Out of scope:
  - Do not change SEO site names, package names, binary names, database records, company-card defaults, routes, API handlers, auth/session logic, object storage, or navigation destinations.

Dependencies between issues
- This batch contains only Issue #236.
- It builds on prior layout behavior where the header logo links to `/` and the footer logo links to `https://ideavibes.ai/`; this issue changes the brand lockup content and accessibility labels only.
- It is independent of post rendering, newsletter submission, admin workflows, sitemap/robots output, Markdown rendering, image uploads, and database migrations.

Key design decisions
- Product brand/name comes from the issue: render the visible lockup as `ideavibes blog`.
- Keep the hosted logo URL and dimensions intact; add the wordmark as sibling text inside the existing brand anchor so the whole lockup remains a single link target.
- Update image `alt` to `ideavibes blog` and surrounding link `aria-label` to match the new lockup. If both the image alt and adjacent visible text are present, verify the resulting accessible name is acceptable in tests and markup.
- Match the current editorial ink/weight/size with local Tailwind classes rather than introducing custom CSS.
- Validate with `npm run backend:test` or `cargo test --manifest-path rust-backend/Cargo.toml`; broader E2E coverage is not required for this narrow template/test update.
